Date: Thu, 18 Nov 93 20:25:06 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: QUERY: NP Completeness

> From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
> I won't feel complete until someone explains NP completeness to me.

Ugh; bad pun.  Anyway...

There is a class, called NP, of problems which can be solved in
polynomial time on a non-deterministic machine.  If you've taken
some CS courses, you've probably seen non-deterministic finite
automata.  Here we use Turing machines or the equivalent, but the
way non-determinism works remains the same.

An example of a problem in NP is the traveling salesbeing problem:
given a complete N-vertex graph with non-negative edge weights, does
there exist a Hamiltonian cycle whose cost is less than a specified
k?  A non-deterministic machine can start at any point, and take
*all* paths at each step, totting up the cost to date.  If any
`thread' comes up with a solution after N steps, it says "yes", so
the whole machine does.  By judicious handwaving, we can see that
this algorithm takes time only linear in N.

O.K., now a technical tool: polynomial-time reducibility.  Question A
is poly-time reducible to question B if there exists a way, requiring
only polynomial time, to transform an input to A into one for B --
such that B's answer is correct for A.  Even more informally, it
means that if you can do B in polynomial time, you can do A too.

Finally, NP-completeness.  A question is NP-complete if it's in NP,
and *every* question in NP can be poly-time reduced to it.  Thus, a
poly-time solution to any NP-complete problem is a solution to all.
Proving that something is NP-complete looks very hard: how do you
deal with everything in NP at once?  Well, Cook did it in 1971 by
essentially building an instance of "the boolean satisfiability
problem" which emulated an arbitrary Turing machine.  Rather slick.
Now you can prove your problem NP-complete by reducing Cook's
problem to it -- or any other known NP-complete problem, of which
there are thousands.

(A question for those in the know: is there a general proof that an
O(f(n)) solution to an NP-complete decision problem can be used in
P(f(n)) time to get a solution to the corresponding optimization
problem?)

> Simon! Levy

