Other drugs, such as DOM, DOB, and N-acetyl-LSD, have been sold as
LSD.  This risk can be minimized by avoiding any dosage form besides
blotter paper.  An active dose of DOB or DOM will almost fit on a
tab -- it's been done in Australia, and the tabs were a full
centimeter on a side, and *crunchy*.  Not hard to recognize.

> I suspect that one batch of marijuana we got was dosed with PCP
> and another with Mescaline.

PCP, possibly.  Mescaline, never.  First, it's very hard to come by,
and quite expensive.  Any "mescaline" sold on the street can be
assumed to be LSD or phencyclidine.  It's an adulteree, not an
adulterant.  Second, it wouldn't work physically.  The active dose
is about a fifth of a gram, approximating the active dose of the
marijuana!

> The only drug that I had any sort of confidence in was mushrooms, which I
> never actually took, because they LOOK like mushrooms, but there are
> probably folks out there selling ordinary mushrooms sprayed with LSD and
> calling them "magic."

This used to happen a lot.  Now that cultivation techniques have
improved, it doesn't seem to happen much.  Nevertheless, I'd suggest
that anybody planning to eat a given mushroom get a book and be able
to positively identify it, or trust someone else who made the
identification.  Who knows where the things came from?

>    If you're interested in prolonging the quality of your mental
> function for as long as possible, taking psychoactive chemicals of
> unknown quality, nature, and strength is a gamble with high stakes.

The legal situation is indeed unfortunate.  (It's worth pointing out
that it's the "unknown" that's the problem, not the "psychoactive"
-- I don't see any particular correlation between psychoactivity and
neurotoxicity or similar hazards.)  The risks can be reduced to
fairly low levels by knowing what you're doing and by making
intelligent choices of compounds and dosage forms.  You may still not
perceive countervailing benefits -- or any benefits at all, for that
matter.

