Date:     Fri, 19 Nov 93 5:17:27 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  How Voluntary Socialism is Disproven Rationally

It's been an interesting study in memetics to see how a few of our members have
answered the information request about how voluntary socialism is rationally
disproven.  Only one approach seems to have been used thus far, one I have
little hope will succeed at being informative: the positivist, it's been tried
and failed one, based on analogy to the methods of the natural sciences.

Of course, this applies to Artificial Intelligence, too, yet many extropians
still seem to believe in that, so they must not apply this standard in the
same way as they do to social systems.

This is only one of three methods of answering the question.  The other two
are the historicist and praxaeologic.  The historicist is even worse than
the positivst, because it amounts to: historical conditions aren't right
for voluntary socialism to work - which leaves unanswered a theory of
history, specification of what those conditions would be, how they might
be achieved or not as the case may be, etc.

If this exhausts one's argumentative repertoire, however, then one will fail
to persuade those who reject the positivist and historicist methods, and
likely resort to causal explanation of their disagreement with you; they're
ignorant, evading reality, stupid, etc.  But if your repertoire includes
more, then you will be able to persuade more people.  Thus, we ought to
consider adding the praxaeological answer:

Voluntary socialism is impossible because without the emergence of the
phenomena of prices from the exchange of private property rights, the
rational economic calculation we need in order to best allocate scarce
resources (means) to produce goods and services (ends) is impossible.

This is a.k.a. the "knowledge problem" that the Austrian school of
economics has developed extensively, beginning with Ludwig von Mises
in the 1920s, continuing with Friedrich von Hayek in the 1930s, to
a reborn and growing field of scholars today.  A recently published
book on the subject is "From Marx to Mises," by David Ramsay Steele,
a former marxist who was converted to libertarianism by this argument.

The conclusion of this argument is that capitalism does a better job
of coordinating the allocation of resources in society than socialism,
even without the State.

I hardly consider this debating basics, because it's never been debated
or hardly even discussed on this list, much less on the newsgroups.  If
you ask me, this is the single most neglected part of the case for
capitalism.

I also find it preferable to the historical method because it rules out
all possible variations of socialism, defined as collective ownership
of capital (the means of production), without having to cover each
and every case that's ever happened and consider whether these exhaust
the possibilities.  The socialist movement of the New Left mainly
advocates a form of socialism that hasn't been practiced much, and thus
has yet to fail in practice: direct-democratic socialism, usually at
the municipal level.  By positivist standards, we'd have to run the
experiment, at cost to human happiness, before ruling it out, then
all the rest of the alternatives socialists could possibly come up
with.

It's not enough to indicate failed socialist institutions in the past;
there are failed capitalist institutions, too.  Even if there are less
market failures in the past, this still says nothing about the future.
Hindsight would seem to be a strange method for futurists to rely on
exclusively for their argumentation.

The praxaeologic argument rules out, based upon irrefutable axioms,
all possible variations upon socialism insofar as productivity goes.

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now!

