From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: LANG: E-prime and General Semantic Swamp

   I probably should read Korzybsky before talking on the subject
(maybe, we want to add the book to the extropian ::readinglist too),
but I'll still risk it:

   I would agree that the way 'be' is usually used, is wrong;
it would annoy me a lot, if I took the standard human concept set
seriously.

   Consider sentences:

"Hi, I *am* Bob".
-----------------
   - that stupid 'magic of names'.  You [Bob] somehow manage to think
that this 'introduces' you or reveals your essence. Pure nonsense.
"My name *is* Bob" makes much more sense.
"Call me Bob" seems still better; it explicitly carries the invitation
to contact that was only implied in the previous two versions; it also
implies a _suggested type_ of relationship (not "Robert [L. Obert]",
Bobby, Bobzilla or BLOb@nowhere that others may know you as).


  "Sasha is a long-haired man".
  ----------------------------

You either use it as a method of identification - then just say it:
"you can tell him by...", or want to say that I have long hair, or...
alas, basic humans tend to 'classify' complex entities by surface
features...
"The projection of this object's structure on my pathetic meme-set
looks like a long-haired-man" would be more appropriate.


  "You are a bad boy".
   -------------------

"Don't pee into my soup any more, or else!" sounds more descriptive,
constructive and pedagogical, as well as reveals an active position
and dynamic optimism of the speaker. ;-)

   -------------------------------------------------------

   In all the above examples, "be" attempted to portray statements
referring to particular events/features as descriptions of the very
complex objects mentioned. 
   This is definitely inadequate, since such descriptions have little
to do with the "essense" of objects, and even in the milder, classifi-
cational, sense are way too assertive, since classifications based
on a small arbitrary set of surface features tend to be interpretational,
oversimplified and/or misleading.
    
    Though such overly assertive statements create a feeling of one's
control over the environment as well as safety and comfort - widely
preferable to the dynamic confusion in the world filled with statists...
   

    HOWEVER,  the same classifying and assertive statements seem to be
quite adequate for describing simple functions, features and entities:

"My e-mail address *is* sasha@cs.umb.edu"       - essense
"This function *is* smooth".                    - feature
"My fingers *are* touching the keyboard now"    - action
"Statists *are* fools"                          - classification of simple
								objects ;-)

   Two recent examples of (non-Prime thinking)-related problems:

 1. Import duties on 'foreign' cars.

   U.S. - Canadian agreement allows free trade in cars produced in the
two countries; however, foreign cars are subject to U.S. import duties.
Now, how much of the car should be made abroad for the whole thing to be
considered 'foreign'?
  The government's semantic gurus consider cars 'domestic' if they [cars]
have 60% or more value created in North America. Cars with higher "alien
contents" are considered 'foreign' and are taxed. This arbitrary threshold
creates multiple *economic* problems.
  The solution?  (within their economic view): Categorize/tax not *cars*,
they are [usually] not [entirely] domestic or foreign; Categorize/tax
the foreign *value contents*!
   Advantages:
     - the "47.3% of the cost of this car was incurred abroad" assertion
	has more semantic validity, objectiveness, and accuracy than
	"this car is foreign".
     - the technological considerations of the car manufacturers are not
	disrupted by a sudden leap in taxes at the 60%/40% value border.
     - manufacturers of *all* cars would have an incentive to decrease
	their foreign value contents, not only of those just over 60/40
