Date: Fri, 19 Nov 93 00:13:28 PST
From: GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov
Subject: Deja vu: Drugs and E-prime

While driving home from work this evening, I was thinking about the
last time the topic of mind-altering chemicals came up on the
Extropians list. It occurred 11 months ago (tis the season to be
jolly!), and there appears to be a more enthusiastic affirmation on
this next go-around of the topic. However, what I remembered next
amused to me no end-

Korb.'s E-prime was discussed at exactly the same time!

What an odd feeling of deja-vu.

The sequence of events 11 months ago was something like this:

I had first stated something about the positive uses of psychoactive
chemicals...

Elizabeth Schwartz had replied: "LSD *is* dangerous...."

I argued that her statement was far too strong, and I thought it was
more accurate to say: "LSD *can be* dangerous..."

And somebody else replied (don't remember his name.. JK?  CP?) "Sounds
like a job for E-prime to me!"

See how neatly I can tie these threads together??  :) :)

So, I have some brief remarks about these two topics.

E-Prime.  When one switches the voice from passive voice to
first-person or active voice, I note that the result is a more truthful
accounting of noun and verb. If the noun is a person performing an
action, then the sequence of events is clear. I much prefer the
statement: "I conducted experiment A" to "Experiment A was conducted."
I have not studied E-prime yet, but the topic made me think of the
world of technical writing, and how the writers/scientists could
improve their communication 100-fold if they followed some of E-prime.
When I took my technical writing course some years ago (required for
Physics graduate students), the teacher was adamant about first-person
writing. (He and I and others like us are bucking the entrenched trend
of passive-voice scientific writing.) He taught us that when one writes
in first person, he/she are taking responsibility for the act
performed. If you see "Experiment A was conducted" in a paper, who do
you go to, to get details?  This style of writing is not only boring to
the reader, but it seems to sweep under the rug, who did what, when,
and how.

Drugs.  Eli B. and I have praised the PIHKAL book before.  I'm
anxiously waiting for the TIHKAL book. Anyone know when it will be out?
I, and several others on this list, have met the Shulgins (authors of
the PIHKAL). They are serious, dedicated scientists, and I have the
utmost respect for them.  I also think that what they're doing in
promoting the positive aspects of psychoactives is very courageous. I
would put Lester Grinspoon in the same category. His texts in the field
(I believe he is a chemist like Shulgin) are supposed to be classics.
He is an old friend of the Shulgins and while I've never met him, I'm
good friends with his son David Grinspoon, a planetary scientist who
used to work at NASA-Ames. (How would you like to grow up in a family
like that? Father, friends, all pioneers in the wonderful world of
altered states.)

Since MDMA, MDA, LSD have all been talked about, let me fill in a hole
about the effects of KETAMINE.  Like Charlie's experience with opium
(I've been very curious about opium myself, not sure that I would have
the courage to try it, though), I did not *intend* to experience this
drug.  It was given to me as an alternative to the general anesthetic
drug I was supposed to receive for a minor surgical operation. I was
told that the reason that I received this alternative was because I had
a cold (fluid in throat, nose etc), and therefore it was too risky to
use the conventional anesthetic. I don't know what is different about
Ketamine that permits this use. My experience occurred 15 years ago,
and I sincerely hope that Ketamine is not still being used in this
fashion. (Does anyone know?) Anyway my experience can be summed up as a
NIGHTMARE. (It's very vivid to me even now.) I awoke from my minor
operation with very loud, compressed sounds in my ears.  I couldn't
open my eyes yet. I couldn't feel my body at all- didn't think I had
one. I heard nurses voices and a person trying to throw up, and it
wasn't until an hour or so later that I realized that the voices were
directed at me, and *I* was the person trying to throw up.  When I
could finally open my eyes, it looked like all of the walls were caving
in on me. I felt literally that I would be crushed by them. I was
terrified. I don't remember much else except that it took a good 2-3
hours to come out of the effects of the Ketamine- I understand that
other anesthetics take considerably less time. So for the life of me, I
can't understand what positive effects John Lilly experienced with
Ketamine.  Maybe all of those hours in the sensory deprivation tank
altered his mind so much, that Ketamine was a walk in the park!  I
wouldn't recommend this drug for *any* kind of transformation
experience.

Amara Graps 
graps@gal.arc.nasa.gov

