Date: Fri, 19 Nov 93 2:08:31 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: E-Prime & thoughts on Extropianism

Sasha writes good examples of where 'to be' can mislead.  But 
even the following benign-sounding uses can also mislead:

> "My e-mail address *is* sasha@cs.umb.edu"       - essense

E-mail addresses, like practically everything else, are not an 
essence.  For example my main e-mail address is (since August 1993)
szabo@netcom.com, but you could also reach me at szabo@techbook.com,
and perhaps other places.  You can also reach me by mailing to 
extropians or cypherpunks (quite rude for personal mail, but it 
would work).  If you fed "Nick's e-mail address *is* szabo@netcom.com"
into a strictly deductive argument, the outcome might well be garbage 
(eg "I forgot Nick's address on netcom, therefore I should give up trying
to reach him").

My point is not to ding folks for using 'is' in these cases -- it's mighty
convenient, and it often sounds silly when you try to avoid it --
just to point out that if extrapolated into deductive
logic 'is' creates the same problems of overgeneralization  that many
of the other uses of 'is' you sited create.  If you use 'is', don't
take it too seriously, as in "nay, not seems, it *is*, goddamn
it!" (Sorry Bill Shakespeare).

> "This function *is* smooth".                    - feature

Depending on the function, it's more precise to say
"This function is smooth within the XYZ system of axioms".

> "My fingers *are* touching the keyboard now"    - action

This simply modifies the verb "touch".  The only example I can think
of where this would mislead is if you fed that sentence 
to an expert system for timing your keystrokes.

> "Statists *are* fools"                          - classification of simple
> 								objects ;-)

If it were even remotely close to being true, the world would be in much 
better shape.  The foolishness of one's political opinions bears very
little correlation to the foolishness of dealings in ones personal
life (jobs, relationships, gaining power and wealth, etc.)  As
evidence, I can point to very successful capitalists whose political
opinions are very socialist (eg Felix Rohatyn), and I can point
to large doses of foolishness in some relationships in the
libertarian/extropian community.  The slanderous diatribes 
between factions in the cryonics and smart drugs communities are two 
extremely unfortunate examples.  I readily admit to falling short
in some of these areas myself, alas.  I may even share an
"individualist" personality type that links habits of (and corresponding
beliefs in) individualism with some kind of asociality, perhaps due to 
underemphasis of empathy, cooperation, altruism, love, or other 
important aspects of human behavior that don't fit neatly into the 
"rational self-interest" model.  Rational self-interest is fine 
for describing relationships between strangers and larger groups, but
(in any reasonably parsimonious form) it plays a much lesser role
in effective personal relationships.  I even expect to get flamed
in such asocial ways for daring to make this observation, alas.
(Perhaps my making these observations is itself asocial, who knows --
I don't claim to be exempt from this unfortunate phenomenon!)

Digressing a bit more along these lines, the issue of political vs. 
personal foolishness relates to my biggest gripe about the Extropian 
movement (which is otherwise quite admirable), is that we spend too
much time idealizing the free market and too little time figuring 
out how to succeed in it or helping each other do so.
Discussions of that nature on this list are often treated as viscious 
competitions instead of cooperative explorations, and are slighted in 
favor of personally useless political debates.  Personally useless,
because the viability of one's politcal opinions about how society
as a whole shoud[n't] be run has practically no bearing on one's success 
in personal self-transformation, life extension, and other personal 
Extropian goals.  (For that matter, the accuracy of libertarian
argument doesn't even bear much relation to its viability in today's
politcal arena, eg in electoral success, but that's a whole 'nother
can of worms).  

Some value this list as a "safe haven" for political beliefs, but
to me that's not very valuable.  It's not of much value to the
political movement itself, either.  Political beliefs are meant to be 
spread; they're useless if confined.  What I'd really like to see 
is a safe haven for cooperation in achieving more personal kinds
of Extropian goals.  Conducting one's life and choosing relationships
primarily based on political belief is an extremely narrow way
to go.  If that's what the Extropian movement is primarily about it's
not going to get very far, and we're going to see more people
drop off, like Hans Moravec, Keith Henson, Hal Finney, Robin Hanson, etc.,
(and for a while Tim May, Charlie Stross, etc.), to readjust their
lifestyle to a better balance between the personal and the idealistic.  
We will also tend to drive off people because they don't
share this or that specific idealistic belief (eg we've driven off
libertarians who don't share our optimism about technology,
anarchists who believe there will be many altruism-based communes
in an anarchy, etc.), even though they might share many personal
Extropian goals as well as at least some important idealistic ones.

As many folks know I've written quite voluminously (and with
high quality, according to most of my feedback) to Usenet advocating
anarcho-capitalism and many other Extropians idealistic goals, eg
space travel, and am now engaged in designing and writing software for the 
crypto-anarchy movement.  However, merely sharing these
goals makes a poor basis for freindship, love, personal success, etc. 
if said people are not also committed to sharing personal goals and
styles of relationship, which often bear little relation to these 
idealistic goals.  Of course there are gray areas between the idealistic 
and the personal, such as cryonics and the commercial aspects of 
crypto-anarchy, and such areas might be the most fruitful for Extropians
to emphasize.  But the normal stuff of contemporary human
success (love, wealth, etc.), without which we fail to 
reach transhuman or posthuman success, should have top priority.

