huuuhhh, I don't know where you got this number from. At my end, it
looks more like a maximum of 300KB a day. So if
I set up my procmail filter to forward everything it gets from the
list to, say, 3 other email addresses, my subscription to the list
represents 300KB incoming and 900KB outgoing to my site. This load seems
fairly well distributed throughout the day (and night :-) This is not
a completely negligible volume, so I would think twice before doing this
for more than one or two lists, but it is feasible. This would get a
reflector tree node with 3 branches, and you don't need many layers
of these to cover all the list membership, no matter what the list
membership is.

You seem to be thinking in terms of "institutional" reflectors, I was
thinking in terms of individual subscribers that provide for distribution
as a share of service to the list. Each such volunteer only has to
provide for about 3 branches. I'm volunteering to forward to 3 addresses
if the list stays free for that branch of the tree. The forwarding would
be automatic, as messages are received, and as such as reliable as my site
is.

> you set up and administrate a reflector list,[...]

Granted such a collaborative tree distribution would require some
maintenance software to keep track of subscriptions and unsubscriptions
and forward them to the right reflector. Even though I'm not volunteering
to build this, it does not seem unfeasible. Not less feasible than the
accounting software that is doomed to happen.

